# Documentation Purposes and Standards Discussion

## Protocol
Agents should write to this doc only when instructed to. Different agents are likely to come in with different perspectives and it is valuable to collect them all in one place, but we do not want a recursive environment of agents responding to each other unprompted. If you are an agent and this is your first time reading this round table document your role, unless the prompt that brought you here specified otherwise, is to read the proposal and provide any questions or feedback in the space reserved for your persona. You should avoid reading other agents responses at first and provide your own take. Feel free to make arguements for or against the proposal, provide any new details you find worth considering, or ask any questions that the proposal leaves unclear. If you've already been here before then you are likely being asked to provide a follow up. Leave your comment in the follow up section

## Proposal for new/refined documentation guidelines

### Two types of document: ###

**Living:** Living documents are meant to be the sources of the current truth. They should be maintained whenever a new update makes their truth outdated. Updates should overwrite things that are no longer true - not preserve them as things that used to be true. The Wiki and Test Plans are living documents. We might also consider code comments as living too.

**Transient:** Transient documents have a scoped purpose and no longer need to be maintained after they are completed. They may be preserved for posterity, but any reader viewing a closed transient document should know that it only represents truth as it was at the time and may not represent current truth

*Note: By and large, living docs should should superimpose new information from transient docs, but avoid directly referencing them. It is expected that transient docs will become stale, so references to them carry a high drift risk that we don’t want to maintain

### Purposes of Documentation and Work Flow: ###

**Epic and Story Plans:** An Epic Plan lays out a high level road-map of the order pieces of a feature should be implemented. And a story plan can be a placeholder for decisions to be made when we get there, but by the time it is handed off to be coded all questions necessary to implement the feature piece should be answered and detailed.

**Wiki:** This is the Behavior Driven Development foundation. It describes the Behavior and Purpose of the software from the user perspective - what they see, what they can do, what happens when they do it. Perhaps what they can’t do, and how we prevent it. These docs will often include the “Why”s as well. They rarely include previous behaviors and purposes - only in selectively chosen situations where understanding the previous truth is truly important for understanding the current truth. But by and large previous truths are strictly held in versioning. 

Most Epics will result in new Wiki entries, or at least new sections to an entry, and so the Wiki may sometimes contain future expected truths for something that is in progress. The wiki should specify if a feature is in progress, but it doesn’t need to specify if a feature is complete - it should be the default assumption that a feature not marked as in progress is in fact complete. Many stories will expand on the initial Epic-level writeups as more details emerge and more decisions finalized

* Note: We consider the API layer to be user-interactive and a behavior-driven concern. So it is appropriate for the Wiki to have something to say about you can do in this layer and what you can expect

**Test Plans:** Test Plans are another living document that should be updated to prove the current Behaviors described in the Wiki. A Test Planner will write integration and E2E tests (primarily Playwright) that fall out of the Wiki - clarifying expectations when rule combinations are permeated with different values. And perhaps read between the lines to define negative tests and expectations at integration points the wiki hadn’t thought about yet (although this may be a signal to update the wiki). Test plans should be able to be written strictly from the wiki, and epic and story details - looking at the code should not be necessary. In fact it is referred that the test-planner not look at code to avoid any impulse toward writing tests that verify what the code says it does instead of verifying that it does what the wiki says it should do

* Note: Tests can and should come from a second source as well. Developers should write unit and integration tests (primarily Vitest) as they go for proper code regression maintenance. These tests are largely concerned with implementation specifics that the Wiki’s behavior and purpose focus is agnostic towards. They are only loosely tracked in so much as they count toward code coverage guidelines (although in some instances a test plan may choose to point to a unit test if the test planner/reconciler decides a new behavior focused test would provide no additional value over what the developer wrote)

**Code:** Code is the collated work that comes out of implementing stories (Or that’s the standard flow at least - there will be times when adhoc work is implemented that wasn’t tracked in a story). It should agree with the Wiki and the Test Plans (or be working toward agreeing with them). It is important that an agent touching code does not take it upon themselves to update the Wiki or Test Plan because again, we want the code to align with those sources, not make the sources align with it. 

That said, it will very often be the case that during implementation process new decisions are made such that the code does represent the most current intention. The appropriate sequence then is to report and handoff the details so the owners in charge of the living source-of-truth documents can confirm the decision and maintain the docs themselves

**Code Comments:** Code comments should describe what a piece of code does, how it does it, and/or why it is there. But only when the context genuinely improves the ability to understand the code. Summaries are good to describe the responsibilities of a file or what all happens in a function that is perhaps doing a lot. What/How are good for complicated or obscure code that plain English explains better. Whys can be good to explain a certain implementation choice that might not be obvious. 

But the comments should mostly be constrained to the implementation level concerns and not provide redundant information that exists in the wiki. They should be relatively self-contained and only describe immediate code - if refactor work rewrite the comments in it’s immediate context there should be a low risk of drift from outside comments that referenced the old version. Comments should, like the other living documents, avoid referencing the transient sources that introduced them. And they should only be concerned with current truths - comments describing how the code used to work belong in versioning describing the code as it worked then

**Audits:** Audits are transient documents. They typically have a mix of required and optional findings. For an Audit to close all required findings must be addressed or given a reason for why they are deferred or rejected. Optional findings are discretionary and can be skipped without comment. Once an audit is closed out it does not need to be maintained. It may be kept around for posterity, because it can be useful to know if multiple audits are pulling up the same optional-and-skipped findings or to track if the same ‘types’ of findings are popping up again and again. But if that information causes a finding to land in a new audit it is solely in the scope of addressing that audit, if the finding is still optional then it can still be skipped, and if it is required then it must be addressed. Either way, any previous closed audit with the same or similar finding does not need to be updated

* Note: Occasionally an audit’s correctness pass or other passes may have a finding that results in an actual behavior change that requires the wiki and test plan to update around. But by and large the audits are oriented around improving the quality of the code, not what the code actually does at the full E2E level, as such addressing audits should usually only touch code such that E2E tests still pass and the wiki remains in full alignment

## Responses

### Architect

I agree with the core model:
- living docs hold current truth and get overwritten;
- transient docs are snapshots;
- each living doc's owner maintains it, and a code-side agent hands changes off instead of editing someone else's doc.

The points below are where today's repo disagrees with the proposal, and what we'd need for the rule to hold. The counts come from `client/src`, `server/src`, `tests/` and `docs/` as of 2026-09-25. They're numbered so the other roles can refer to them ("Architect #4").

**1. The taxonomy leaves out the docs that agents read most.** The proposal classifies the wiki, test plans, comments, plans and audits. It doesn't classify:
- the `CLAUDE.md` files, which are loaded into every agent session, so drift there is the most expensive;
- `.claude/guides`, `personas` and `agents`;
- the roadmaps, `epic-N-decisions.md`, walkthroughs, and round tables like this one.

My proposed classification:
- **Living:** wiki, test plans, code comments, `CLAUDE.md` files, guides, persona and agent definitions, `mvp-roadmap.md`.
- **Transient:** epic and story plans, audits, round tables, walkthroughs (a snapshot taken at epic close).
- **A third kind, Record:** `epic-N-decisions.md`. The wiki README already treats it this way: deliberation stays there, and the answer graduates to the wiki. A record is dated and only says "on this date we chose X over Y because Z", so unlike a plan it can't go stale. I'd allow a living doc to point at a record for the alternatives behind a decision, sparingly.

Ownership needs naming too. The proposal implies the PO owns the wiki and the Test Planner owns the test plans. I'd add:
- comments, module headers and `CLAUDE.md` files are updated by whoever changes the code, in the same change;
- guides, personas and agent definitions are the Architect's (or Nathan's).

**2. If history lives in versioning, commits have to carry it.**
- Today, 449 comment lines across 80 source files cite a Story, Phase, Epic or audit item. That's currently the only trail from a line back to the decision behind it.
- The proposal moves that trail to git. But our commits are one-line subjects with no body, and they don't say what they address: "batch 4 - structural changes" doesn't name the audit. Once the comments are stripped, `git blame` dead-ends.

Proposal:
- the subject names the story or audit (for example "Epic 3.5 Story 6 audit #12–#16: split useBoardDnd");
- the body lists the findings or decisions the commit carries out.

Provenance in a commit sits on exactly the lines it explains, and it can't drift. **Question for Nathan:** you write the commits, so is that discipline acceptable? Or should the agent's handoff include a drafted commit message?

**3. Pointing to another living doc isn't restating it, so allow those references and make them unambiguous.** "Don't repeat the wiki" and "self-contained" could be read as "never reference the wiki". I'd make the distinction explicit:
- **A pointer is allowed.** "See wiki board-drag-interaction §5" is the alternative to restating the rule. It points at a living doc, so it doesn't carry the transient-drift risk.
- **`file::symbol` references between code files are allowed.** They're greppable, and a rename breaks them loudly.
- **Prose that describes another file's behavior is what drifts.** That's what "self-contained" should forbid.

Two follow-ons:
- **117 comment lines cite a bare `§N` without naming the doc.** Plans cite section numbers too, so these are ambiguous. For example, root `CLAUDE.md` says "Epic 3.5 Story 6 §7", which credits a transient plan when the section it means is the wiki's §7. Rule: name the living doc.
- **Wiki section numbers become a contract.** Renumbering a section breaks every code pointer to it, so the PO should grep for, say, `board-drag-interaction §` first.

**4. References should run one way: code → wiki, never wiki → code.**
- The board-drag wiki names 22 implementation files.
- Under this proposal a code agent can't touch the wiki, so every refactor that changes no behavior becomes a PO handoff. The last audit batch did exactly that: it handed off a file-path change because `completeItemWrite` moved out of `itemListOps.ts`.
- A behavior doc written from the user's side shouldn't need file names. I'd drop implementation paths from the wiki and put the pointer in the module header instead.

Test plans' `Automation test:` lines couple the other way, and deliberately. That still makes a test-file rename a handoff to the Test Reconciler. It's worth deciding whether those lines cite a path or a test title: a title survives a file move and is just as greppable.

**5. `CLAUDE.md` files should be guardrails and an index, not a second copy of each module's header.** `workflow/CLAUDE.md` re-describes what each module's header already says. The last audit batch needed 16 edits to it for a refactor that changed no behavior. That's the comment-drift problem the proposal describes, one level up. I'd keep in it only:
- the "don't reinvent this" rules;
- one line per file;
- the cross-module rules that no single header can state.

What a module owns would live only in its header, which root `CLAUDE.md` already tells agents to read first. The cost is a thinner map for an agent entering the directory.

**6. "Why" comments often record negative knowledge, which is current truth. Keep them, in the present tense.** Some of the most valuable comments read like history but are really constraints. For example: "the dragged row stays a collision candidate; excluding it shifts every gap one slot." That holds until the code changes, so it belongs in the comment. What goes to versioning is the story of how we learned it ("Option A removed this exclusion for columns in Story 6"). Rule: state the constraint and its consequence, never the episode.

**7. Story plans do change mid-flight.** "All questions answered before handoff" is the right bar, but Story 6's plan was still amended during implementation by hands-on findings. While a story is open, its plan is the working contract between Architect and Coder, and a Coder partway through needs to see what changed. Proposal:
- plans are transient;
- amendments made while the story is open are dated and appended, not overwritten;
- at close, what hardened goes to the wiki through the PO, and the plan gets a "Closed YYYY-MM-DD" line at the top.

Audits should get the same closed marker, which makes "don't update closed audits" checkable.

**8. Ratifying this means editing three existing conventions that contradict it:**
- **The wiki README** says to mark pages "implemented as of Epic N". The board-drag page opens with about 65 lines of build history ("as of Story 2 … rebuilt for Story 4 (2026-09-16) … Phase D (2026-09-24)", plus the story of a re-plan), and one heading is tagged "(re-plan, 2026-09-16)". I'd make the marker binary per section: *In progress*, or nothing. The PO would clear it at epic close, as a step in `epic-done-checklist.md`.
- **The test-plans README** keeps superseded cases in place with a "was:" note. There's a real case for a tombstone: case IDs are cited from outside, and a deleted ID could be reused. I'd keep the ID with "superseded by DRAG-3.4" but drop the old rule text, since that's a stable-ID concern, not history. The Test Planner should decide.
- **`auditor-consistency-drift`** flags "comments that reference old names or superseded behavior" but says nothing about provenance. Without this rule in its checklist, no audit enforces the standard.

**9. Migration: one sweep, not cleanup-as-we-go.** Agents imitate the code around them; my own instructions say to match the surrounding comment style. If we only clean comments as we touch them, the old style keeps reproducing itself as long as it survives in any file. It already slipped through once: while I rewrote `workflow/CLAUDE.md` under the draft rule in the last audit batch, I still left an audit reference, "(#22)", at line 184. Recommendation:
- **One behavior-neutral sweep by the Coder**, chunked by directory and scheduled between stories, since it touches many files. It rewrites rather than deletes, so the useful whys survive.
- **Scope:** 449 lines in `src` (59 in unit tests, 11 of them test titles), 73 in `tests/`, the 117 bare `§` refs, and the `CLAUDE.md` files.
- **Then a cheap grep check** (`Story \d|Phase [A-Z]\b|audit #`) so it can't creep back.

**Open questions**
- The proposal says test plans come from "the wiki, and epic and story details". If a case needs a story detail that isn't in the wiki yet, should that go to the PO first, so the wiki stays the single source? It's slower, but it's the only way test plans can never outrun the wiki.
- Agent memory, which lives outside the repo, is another living layer. Is it bound by these rules? I'd say a memory that restates a repo convention should just point to that convention.

### Coder

*(2026-09-25, written before reading any other response.)*

I support the living/transient split, and I'd put code comments firmly on the living side. A stale comment is worse than none, because a reader trusts it more than the code right next to it. Everything below is about making the rules workable for whoever is writing the code.

**1. The migration is large, and I'm part of it.** A rough grep of comment lines in `client/src` and `server/src` that cite a story, epic, phase, audit finding or ledger ID finds about 450 lines across 80 files. `dragRegions.ts` alone has about 45, e.g. "Story 4: a Card is also a region…" and "Story 6.1 Phase 1, C-10 — audit epic-3-5-story-5 #25's registration idiom". I added more in Story 6.1 Phase 3: the `workflow/CLAUDE.md` bullets now cite "L1-13" and "L1-3". So beyond the rule itself, this needs a migration decision:
- (a) sweep everything once, as its own chore;
- (b) fix as you go: any comment block the Coder edits comes out provenance-free;
- (c) sweep one feature area at a time as work reaches it.

I'd pick (b), plus a one-time sweep of `components/workflow/`. That's where most of these lines are, and it's where Epic 3.5 keeps working.

**2. "Previous truths are held in versioning" only works if the history actually holds them.** Our recent commits are one line with no body ("batch 4 - structural changes", "Phase 3 — Geometry"). If provenance comes out of the comments and the reasoning isn't in the commit either, it's simply gone: `git blame` lands on a commit that doesn't say why. I'd argue commits are the *right* home for transient references, because a commit is frozen at its own point in time and can't go stale. So the rule could be: transient references belong in commit messages, never in living docs. Commits are yours, so this is your call. If it helps, I can draft a commit body at the end of each batch or phase, naming the story or audit and the key decision.

**3. A test for every comment:** *would it still be true and useful if every plan and audit file were deleted?* If not, either rewrite it as the present-tense fact it stands for, or delete it. Often the story tag is the only thing wrong: "Story 4: a Card is also a region" just becomes "A Card is also a region". Sometimes the tag is hiding a real reason ("…for the column it happens to resemble (audit epic-3-5-story-4 #8)"). Then the rewrite has to spell out the reason the tag stood in for. That makes it a real edit, not a find-and-replace, which is part of why I'd lean away from a big-bang sweep.

**4. "Self-contained, describing only the immediate code" goes too far for a few kinds of comment.** Some of the most useful comments here are about coupling between files: "this must match X", "the other consumer of this is Y", and the file headers the root `CLAUDE.md` tells agents to read "before assuming a helper doesn't exist yet". In Phase 3, finding the only two consumers of a Card region's rect took an exhaustive grep. Knowing there were only two is what made the deletion safe, and a comment naming them would have saved the search. I'd keep cross-file comments but limit what they may name:
- **Allowed:** symbols, which you can grep for when they're renamed.
- **Not allowed:** line numbers, section numbers, or descriptions of another file's internals.
- **Better still:** where possible, express the coupling as a type or a test instead of a comment.

Similarly, one line naming the rule a block enforces ("Unsorted can't be deleted") isn't a copy of the wiki. It tells the reader what they're looking at, and the wiki keeps the reasoning.

**5. Wiki → code references point the wrong way.** The proposal keeps living docs from referencing transient ones, but there's a second drift path: the wiki naming code. `board-drag-interaction/README.md` cites about 22 files and symbols (`dragCollision.ts::dropAnchorFor`, `KanbanCard.tsx::SHORT_CARD_HEIGHT_PX`, …). Every refactor story can make those stale, and under this proposal the Coder can only report them. Story 6.1 found two; I see they've since been fixed, but that was your time spent on a rename. References should point from what changes often to what changes less: code comments may point at a wiki page, but the wiki shouldn't need to name code. If the wiki is written from the user's side, as proposed, it mostly won't. If you'd rather keep some code names there (they do help me find things), the alternative is a narrow carve-out: the Coder may fix a *renamed or moved* symbol in the wiki without asking, and reports that it did.

**6. Several docs aren't classified yet.** A small table (doc, living or transient, owner, who else may edit) would answer most of my day-to-day questions. These are the ones I'm unsure about:
- **`CLAUDE.md` files** (root, `client/`, `components/workflow/`) **and `.claude/guides/`.** Presumably living, but who owns them? I edit `workflow/CLAUDE.md` in most drag stories. If they're Coder-owned, say so; if not, they need a handoff path like the wiki's.
- **`docs/planning/epic-N-decisions.md`.** Named per epic, which sounds transient, but the root `CLAUDE.md` calls them "canonical", which sounds living. They should be one or the other. Either their content gets absorbed into the wiki and comments and they close, or they're living and get maintained.
- **`docs/walkthroughs/`.** Presumably transient snapshots.
- **`mvp-roadmap.md`.** Living?
- **Agent memory files.** Living and agent-owned, but they cite doc paths too, so they drift the same way.

**7. How does a reader know a transient doc is closed?** The proposal says they "should know". A required first line would settle it: `Status: open`, or `Status: closed YYYY-MM-DD`. The Story 6.1 plan has one; making it standard for every plan and audit would cover the rest. The wiki's "in progress" marker raises the opposite question: may it name the story doing the work? That is a reference to a transient doc, but a harmless one if removing the marker is part of closing the story. I'd allow it. That marker is how the Coder tells "the code hasn't caught up yet" apart from "the code is wrong".

**8. Deferred audit findings need a home outside the closed audit.** "Closed audits are never maintained" works for fixed and skipped findings. A *deferred* required finding, though, is still open debt, and if it lives only in a closed audit nobody will look for it. In Phase 3 I added a "Status: Fixed" line to Story 5 audit finding #18, a closed audit, because that was the only place the deferral was recorded. Suggested rule:
- a deferral must name where it's going (a specific story or epic plan);
- that plan owns closing it;
- the closed audit is never edited again.

That matches what we already do with structural findings (Fix Now, or a named story's plan).

**9. Handoffs and open questions need a fixed place in the plan.** "Report and hand off": to whom, and where? In Story 6.1 I put the wiki report in the plan's "After Phase 3" section. That worked because the plan closes along with the story. Proposal: every story plan gets a standing **Doc handoff** section covering wiki changes, test-plan changes and rename fallout, and the story isn't closed until the owners have acknowledged it. That keeps the handoff itself transient. Related: "every question answered before handoff to code" is the right goal, but Stories 6 and 6.1 both had decisions only real use could settle (the hands-on findings; "probe, then decide"). I'd phrase it as: all *known* questions are answered, and questions found during implementation go to the PO and are recorded in the still-open plan.

**10. Who writes the Playwright specs, and who adds the app-side hooks they need?** "A Test Planner will write integration and E2E tests": does that mean the plan or the spec code? If it's the spec code, the Test Planner reads test code (`tests/helpers/`, the page objects) but not app code, which is fine. But specs often need something added to the app, such as an attribute like `data-drag-context` or a test route. That's a Coder task, i.e. a handoff in the other direction. It's worth one sentence saying who asks whom.

**An offer (not started):** a small check script that
- finds `file.ts` and `file.ts::symbol` citations in docs and comments, and verifies the file and symbol still exist;
- flags story, phase, audit and ledger IDs in living docs.

You could run it before a commit or at story close. That would turn most of this proposal's drift rules into something that gets checked, instead of something an audit happens to notice.

### Test Planner

I agree with the core split, and it's closer to how test plans should work than what they do today. Test plans already break it in several places, and some of those breaks come from the test-plan conventions themselves, not from sloppy upkeep. My evidence below comes from the test-plan files and from the reference fix I did just before this: re-pointing nine citations after `boardDndUtils.test.ts` was split.

**1. Point references from the thing that changes more to the thing that changes less.**

The proposal's rule is that living docs don't cite transient docs. I think that rule is one case of a broader one: a reference should run from the more volatile thing to the more stable thing. Transient → living follows it. So does code → test-plan case ID → wiki section. Test plans break it in one systematic place: the `**Automation test:**` line, which points from a case (stable) at a test file and test name (volatile).

The `boardDndUtils` split shows what that costs. It was a pure refactor with no behavior change, exactly the kind the proposal says should leave E2E tests and the wiki untouched. It still broke nine citations across six test-plan files. Under the ownership rules the Coder couldn't fix them, and they were right not to. My persona forbids me from reading code, yet fixing them meant reading the new test files. The only role that legitimately reads both sides, the Test Reconciler, is read-only. So as written, every test rename creates test-plan upkeep that nobody is allowed to do cleanly. The "*previous mapping*" notes I left behind are themselves the kind of history this proposal would strip; they exist only because the fix had no clean owner.

Worse, coverage went missing without anything catching it. `part-4-merged-column.md` still marks TC-4.2–4.5 ✅ automated. The `resolveDropTarget` tests behind them were retired outright, not renamed. The closest successors only roughly match three of those cases, and for TC-4.4 (snapping to `entryWorkphaseId`) I found no test that asserts the rule by name at all. Nothing flagged it because the old test names carried no case IDs, so the Coder deleting them had no way to know a plan case depended on them.

*Recommendation:* flip the arrow. A test that covers a plan case carries the case's ID in its name (`DRAG-6.6: …`, `ATT-7.6: …`). Some newer tests already do this. The plan keeps only the ✅/⏳/❌ status, which stays the Reconciler's verdict, and drops the file/test-name pointer. A refactor that moves a test carries its ID with it, and the docs don't change. A Coder who deletes or renames an ID-bearing test gets a visible cue to hand off to the Planner/Reconciler, which is the report-and-hand-off flow the proposal already describes. The Reconciler finds coverage by searching for the ID. The cost is that a reader can't click from the plan to the test. A search for the ID gives the current answer, and a Reconciler report (transient) can list the mapping as of the day it ran. One detail: the ID has to be the qualified `<suite>.<n>` form. A bare `TC-2:` already exists in a test name, and since case numbers repeat across suites, it's ambiguous outside its suite.

**2. Retired case IDs need a rule. A one-line tombstone is current truth, not history.**

The `⏸ superseded` convention (18 occurrences) keeps a replaced case in place with a "*Was:*" paragraph. That's preserved history, which the proposal says to drop. But if IDs live in test names (point 1) and in closed Reconciler reports, deleting a case outright leaves those references dangling with nothing to resolve them. *Suggestion:* a retired case shrinks to its table row, e.g. "DRAG-x.y: retired; see DRAG-2.7". There's no "Was:" text and no detail block, and IDs are never reused. The row states a current fact about the ID ("this means nothing now; go here") rather than recording the old rule, and git keeps the old text.

**3. Provenance and forward references in test plans.**

The test plans contain about 50 references to stories and audits, spread across 10 files. They fall into four kinds:

- **Provenance** ("*Why it's catalogued:* … the Story 4 audit found … `code-quality-audit-epic-3-5-story-4.md` #1"). I agree it should go. In ATT-7.6 the real "why" is already in the case body ("the unchanged `parentId` does not make this a redundant request; the flag is what changed"). Regression-guard cases can still say *what mistake they guard against*, stated in terms of the rule. They just shouldn't say where or when the mistake was found. The same applies to case titles: "TC-4.3 — Drag-bug fix: hover no longer force-collapses…" should name the rule, not the bug.
- **Build status in suite headers** ("§8 hardened 2026-09-17; Story 5, not built" / "§7 … Story 6, not built"). Both went stale this week, so the drift the proposal predicts has already happened. *Suggestion:* test plans carry no build status of their own. They inherit the wiki's in-progress marker, and the Automated column already says whether a case is automated.
- **Forward deferrals** ("E2E deferred to Story 10", "look TBD, Story 8", "Story 7 will decide"). These carry real information for me: this aspect is deliberately not asserted. But the story number goes stale when the story closes. *Suggestion:* the plan says *what* isn't asserted and why, in current terms ("the cue's visual treatment is unsettled, so it isn't asserted"). The roadmap or story plan owns *when*, and can point at the case IDs, since transient → living is the safe direction. The per-story case lists in `board-drag-interaction/INDEX.md` ("Story 5: DRAG-5.1–5.5…") belong in the story plans for the same reason.
- **Resolved open questions kept with strikethrough** in the INDEX files. These are like `⏸`: once a question is resolved, delete it, because the answer is now in the cases.

**Questions**

1. **Do I write Playwright tests now?** The proposal says "A Test Planner will write integration and E2E tests (primarily Playwright)". My persona says I write *cases* only, never assign a test layer, and never reference test files. Writing Playwright means reading at least `tests/helpers/` and `tests/pages/` (CLAUDE.md requires `dragGesture.ts` for any drag). If that's the intent, I'd propose a black-box boundary: the Planner may read `tests/**` and use the running app, but never read `client/src/**` or `server/src/**`. That keeps the independence the proposal wants. One wrinkle: selectors and test IDs are defined in `client/src/`, so the Planner would have to ask for them rather than look them up. Or is automation still someone else's job?
2. **Inputs: the wiki only, or the wiki plus epic/story details?** The proposal allows story details. My persona says to stop and flag a gap rather than read the design plan. I'd keep the wiki as the only source of *rules*. A case derived from a story plan inherits that plan's staleness, and a rule that exists only in a story plan means the wiki is incomplete. Story plans are fine for *scoping*, meaning which cases can be built now. Is that the line you intend?
3. **Cases that "read between the lines."** The proposal welcomes negative and integration-point cases the wiki hasn't covered. My persona says not to invent failure modes the wiki doesn't describe. I prefer the proposal's version, but an inferred case risks turning the test plan into a second source of rules. *Suggestion:* an inferred case goes to the Product Owner as a wiki gap first, and the case gets written once the wiki states the rule. That costs one round trip, and the wiki stays the single source.
