I put both names there myself.

Applying the test:
- **Stay:** API field names (`parentId`, `completedAt`). The API is a behavior surface, per the proposal.
- **Stay:** tuning-panel value names (`popDelayMs`). The panel is something the user touches.
- **Go:** function names, file paths, and DOM/test attributes. Those belong in module headers and comments.

This rule is also what makes the Audits note enforceable. An audit fix that keeps E2E green can't make the wiki false if the wiki names nothing the fix could rename.

**3. Keep reasons, drop provenance.** Much of what reads as a "why" in today's wiki is really who and when.
- "Ruled by the PO 2026-09-24" is provenance, so it goes.
- "A shortCard would cover the Descendants section" is a reason, so it stays.

When a rejected option matters, phrase it as *why not X*, not *it used to be X*. That stays true as long as the rule does, and it stops the next agent from proposing X again. I'd offer this as the test for the proposal's "selectively chosen situations" where earlier behavior belongs in the wiki.

**4. Status is marked per passage, with a two-word vocabulary.** The "unmarked means built" default only works if every unbuilt passage is marked when it's written. In practice it is, because I write ratified rules in before the build. That's also what lets the Test Planner work in parallel with the Coder. I've needed exactly two markers:
- **Planned:** decided, not yet built. It names the current passage it will replace, if any. Example: §6's zoom-in rule replaces §3's "resting on one of that column's own Cards". Until the build, the Test Planner needs both: the current rule is what tests assert. Without the pairing, the two read as a contradiction.
- **Provisional:** the behavior is decided, but the look is a placeholder. Example: the zoom-in cue's dashed outline, which Story 8 restyles.

"Tunable" is different. It's a permanent property, not a status: feel-tuned values stay adjustable and tests shouldn't pin them. So it's a standing note on the value, never a marker that gets removed.

Page-level status headers should go. The one I added to board-drag ("Built so far in Story 6…") was stale within a day. The wiki README's current convention ("implemented as of Epic N" at the top of the page) conflicts with this proposal.

**5. Wiki section numbers are stable anchors.** Test plans, code comments, CLAUDE.md and story docs all cite wiki sections by number. I'll commit to:
- never renumbering;
- inserting new sections as §5a-style;
- leaving a gap when a section is removed.

Code comments may *point* to a wiki section ("own slot is intentionally a candidate — board-drag §2") but not *restate* the rule. The pointer is what stops a well-meaning refactor from "simplifying" away a business rule no unit test covers.

**6. Name the close-out handoff.** "Report and hand off" needs a fixed place. I'd add a **Living-docs handoff** list to every story's closing report:
- rules decided or changed during the build;
- Planned passages now built;
- passages now superseded.

I apply the list to the wiki, and the Test Planner applies it to the test plans. This also replaces the job dates were quietly doing for me. This session I caught my own stale §7 text only because its date predated decision 6 being reopened. Without dates, "is the wiki current?" becomes "was every closed story's handoff applied?". That's a better check anyway.

**7. Closed transient docs say so at the top.** "Any reader should know it only represents truth as it was" needs a mechanism, because agents grep and land in old story docs. One line is enough: *Closed 2026-09-25 — historical; current rules are in the wiki.*

